{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U1pQmTbEqqS4GRXJXGXWoi","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"multi_judge_data_collection\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Collect multi-judge evaluation data on MT-Bench and Arena-Hard using 8 judges. Evaluate 20+ target models with position swap.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"judges_collected\": 8,\n7\t      \"target_models_evaluated\": 22,\n8\t      \"mt_bench_items\": 80,\n9\t      \"arena_hard_items\": 500,\n10\t      \"total_judgments_after_position_swap\": \"~176000\",\n11\t      \"missing_rate_pct\": 4.2,\n12\t      \"logprober_available_judges\": 3,\n13\t      \"logprober_unavailable_key_models\": [\n14\t        \"GPT-5.5\",\n15\t        \"Claude Opus 4.7\",\n16\t        \"Claude Sonnet 4.6\"\n17\t      ],\n18\t      \"api_cost_usd\": 870,\n19\t      \"gpu_hours_used\": 118,\n20\t      \"inter_judge_agreement_fleiss_kappa\": 0.31\n21\t    },\n22\t    \"tables\": [\n23\t      {\n24\t        \"name\": \"Table A1: Judge Coverage Matrix\",\n25\t        \"description\": \"Rows = 22 target models, Columns = 8 judges. Cells = number of items judged. Shows LogProber availability column (✓/✗). Highlights 3 proprietary judges without LogProber access.\\n\"\n26\t      },\n27\t      {\n28\t        \"name\": \"Table A2: Data Collection Summary\",\n29\t        \"description\": \"Per-benchmark breakdown: items, judges, models, total judgments, missing rate, position swap completion rate.\\n\"\n30\t      }\n31\t    ],\n32\t    \"figures\": [],\n33\t    \"analysis\": \"Data collection is sufficient for downstream experiments. The critical weakness is LogProber unavailability for proprietary judges — this forces exp-002 to rely primarily on performance-gap method for these models, increasing e_i estimation variance. The low inter-judge agreement is actually informative: it confirms substantial d_j variation exists, which is part of the paper's motivation. However, it also means the MFRM Stage 1 fit will require more data per judge to achieve stable d_j estimates.\\n\\n\\nData collection itself succeeds — this is infrastructure, not dependent on contamination magnitude. However, two worst-tier signals emerge early: (1) LogProber access is unavailable for the 3 proprietary judges (GPT-5.5, Claude Opus/Sonnet), which are also the highest- capability models most likely to show contamination signal — this cripples the multi-source e_i pipeline for exactly the models that matter most. (2) Inter-judge agreement is low (κ=0.31), reflecting genuine heterogeneity in judge behavior that will make d_j estimation noisier. Missing rate ~4% from API timeouts/refusals is manageable but adds noise.\\n\"\n34\t  },\n35\t  {\n36\t    \"name\": \"item_exposure_estimation\",\n37\t    \"description\": \"Estimate per-item exposure e_i via three-source pipeline: performance gap (primary), temporal signal, LogProber. Combined via weighted composite.\\n\",\n38\t    \"results\": {\n39\t      \"performance_gap_coverage_pct\": 100,\n40\t      \"temporal_signal_coverage_pct\": 35,\n41\t      \"logprober_coverage_pct\": 37.5,\n42\t      \"composite_e_i_mean\": 0.28,\n43\t      \"composite_e_i_std\": 0.19,\n44\t      \"composite_e_i_median\": 0.22,\n45\t      \"performance_gap_noise_std\": 0.18,\n46\t      \"performance_gap_spearman_vs_logprober\": 0.41,\n47\t      \"temporal_spearman_vs_logprober\": 0.38,\n48\t      \"composite_spearman_vs_logprober\": 0.47,\n49\t      \"reference_variant_quality_human_validation_pct\": 82,\n50\t      \"items_with_high_confidence_e_i_pct\": 45,\n51\t      \"mt_bench_avg_e_i\": 0.35,\n52\t      \"arena_hard_avg_e_i\": 0.22\n53\t    },\n54\t    \"tables\": [\n55\t      {\n56\t        \"name\": \"Table 3: Exposure Indicator Source Coverage and Agreement\",\n57\t        \"description\": \"Rows = indicator sources (performance gap, temporal, LogProber, composite). Columns = coverage %, mean e_i, std e_i, Spearman ρ vs LogProber subset. Shows each source's quality and the composite improvement.\\n\"\n58\t      },\n59\t      {\n60\t        \"name\": \"Table A3: Per-Benchmark Exposure Summary\",\n61\t        \"description\": \"MT-Bench vs Arena-Hard breakdown of e_i distribution statistics.\\n\"\n62\t      }\n63\t    ],\n64\t    \"figures\": [],\n65\t    \"analysis\": \"The e_i pipeline produces estimates but with concerning noise levels. The performance-gap method — the only source with full coverage — has ρ=0.41 correlation with the more reliable LogProber signal, meaning roughly 83% of variance is unexplained. This noise propagates directly into Stage 3 via fixed e_i values. The composite improves to ρ=0.47 but cannot overcome the fundamental limitation that LogProber (the best source) is unavailable for proprietary models. This is the critical bottleneck: the models most likely to be contaminated (widely deployed proprietary models trained on internet data) are exactly those for which e_i estimation is least reliable. Under worst tier, this noise is the primary driver of poor c_n recovery and weak ranking improvement.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, the e_i pipeline is noisy because: (1) Performance-gap method has high variance (σ≈0.18) — rephrased reference variants are imperfect controls; difficulty correction via spline introduces its own estimation error; averaging across reference models helps but proprietary models without LogProber anchoring remain uncertain. (2) LogProber is only available for 3/8 judges (open-source ones), covering ~37.5% of judge-model pairs, so the strongest signal source has poor coverage for the most important models. (3) Temporal signal coverage is low (35%) because many items lack clear pre/post-cutoff variant pairs. The composite e_i correlation with LogProber ground truth is only ρ=0.47 — better than any single source but still quite noisy. MT-Bench items show higher average exposure (0.35) than Arena-Hard (0.22), consistent with MT-Bench being older and more widely known, but the overall magnitude is low. Only 45% of items have high-confidence e_i estimates (both performance-gap and at least one other source available), meaning Stage 3 will be fitting with substantial e_i measurement error for over half the items.\\n\"\n66\t  },\n67\t  {\n68\t    \"name\": \"gmfrm_real_data_fit\",\n69\t    \"description\": \"Fit G-MFRM three-stage pipeline on real data. Stage 1: MFRM. Stage 2: e_i from exp-002. Stage 3: Joint gated model. Compare with Chatbot Arena votes.\\n\",\n70\t    \"results\": {\n71\t      \"stage1_mfrm_max_rhat\": 1.02,\n72\t      \"stage1_mfrm_min_ess\": 380,\n73\t      \"stage1_theta_range\": [\n74\t        -1.8,\n75\t        2.1\n76\t      ],\n77\t      \"stage1_d_j_range\": [\n78\t        -0.9,\n79\t        1.1\n80\t      ],\n81\t      \"stage3_max_rhat\": 1.12,\n82\t      \"stage3_min_ess\": 78,\n83\t      \"stage3_divergent_transitions\": 23,\n84\t      \"stage3_c_n_median\": 0.18,\n85\t      \"stage3_c_n_mean\": 0.26,\n86\t      \"stage3_c_n_max\": 0.72,\n87\t      \"stage3_c_n_95ci_width_median\": 0.45,\n88\t      \"stage3_c_n_bimodal\": true,\n89\t      \"stage3_c_n_fraction_near_zero\": 0.68,\n90\t      \"kendall_tau_gmfrm_vs_arena_mt_bench\": 0.84,\n91\t      \"kendall_tau_gmfrm_vs_arena_arena_hard\": 0.86,\n92\t      \"kendall_tau_gmfrm_vs_arena_combined\": 0.85,\n93\t      \"kendall_tau_mfrm_only_vs_arena\": 0.84,\n94\t      \"theta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_correlation\": 0.987,\n95\t      \"runtime_seconds\": 5200,\n96\t      \"graceful_degradation_check\": \"Arena-Hard c_n ≈ 0 subset: G-MFRM θ̂ within 0.02 of MFRM θ̂ — PASS\"\n97\t    },\n98\t    \"tables\": [\n99\t      {\n100\t        \"name\": \"Table 2: G-MFRM Convergence Diagnostics\",\n101\t        \"description\": \"Parameter groups (θ, b, d, c_n) × diagnostics (max R̂, min ESS, mean ESS, divergent transitions). Shows Stage 1 vs Stage 3 comparison. Highlights c_n convergence issues.\\n\"\n102\t      },\n103\t      {\n104\t        \"name\": \"Table 4: Model Ability Estimates and Contamination Scores\",\n105\t        \"description\": \"Rows = 22 target models sorted by θ̂. Columns = θ̂_MFRM, θ̂_GMFRM, Δθ, c_n [95% CI], Arena Elo. Shows most models have Δθ ≈ 0.\\n\"\n106\t      }\n107\t    ],\n108\t    \"figures\": [],\n109\t    \"analysis\": \"The real-data fit confirms worst-tier expectations: contamination exists but is too weak and heterogeneous for G-MFRM to provide meaningful ranking improvement. The bimodal c_n distribution suggests that most current LLMs either (a) genuinely have minimal benchmark contamination, or (b) contamination exists but the e_i pipeline is too noisy to detect it. Convergence issues for near-zero c_n parameters are expected (flat likelihood) but problematic for reporting. The graceful degradation check passes — on the low-contamination subset, G-MFRM correctly reduces to MFRM. Under the reframed narrative, this becomes: \\\"current contamination is detectable but does not yet substantially distort rankings,\\\" positioning G-MFRM as monitoring infrastructure rather than correction tool.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, real contamination is weak and heterogeneous. The fitted c_n distribution is bimodal: ~68% of models have c_n < 0.1 (effectively zero contamination), while a small cluster of 5-7 models shows moderate c_n (0.3-0.72). Median c_n = 0.18 is far below the mvp_002/003 synthetic settings (c~1.0-1.5) and even below mvp_001 (c~0.5). This means the gating mechanism has very little work to do — for most models, the contamination correction term c_n·e_i·g(e_i) is negligible, so G-MFRM θ̂ ≈ MFRM θ̂ (correlation 0.987). Stage 3 convergence is problematic: R̂ = 1.12 for some c_n parameters (the near-zero ones are poorly identified — the posterior is essentially the prior), and ESS = 78 with 23 divergent transitions despite 4000 samples. This occurs because when true c_n ≈ 0, the likelihood surface is flat in the c_n direction (no contamination signal to fit), causing sampling difficulties. The 95% credible intervals for c_n are wide (median width 0.45), meaning individual c_n estimates are not very informative. Kendall-τ with Arena at 0.85 is reasonable but essentially identical to MFRM-only (0.84), showing the contamination correction adds almost nothing to ranking quality.\\n\"\n110\t  },\n111\t  {\n112\t    \"name\": \"baseline_comparison\",\n113\t    \"description\": \"Compare G-MFRM against 8 baselines. Include rank change analysis with top-k stability.\\n\",\n114\t    \"results\": {\n115\t      \"raw_average_tau\": 0.79,\n116\t      \"mfrm_only_tau\": 0.84,\n117\t      \"sequential_tau\": 0.84,\n118\t      \"cyclicjudge_tau\": 0.85,\n119\t      \"cyclicjudge_discard_tau\": 0.85,\n120\t      \"dcr_factor_tau\": 0.82,\n121\t      \"uda_tau\": 0.86,\n122\t      \"pref_leakage_tau\": 0.83,\n123\t      \"gmfrm_tau\": 0.85,\n124\t      \"gmfrm_spearman\": 0.91,\n125\t      \"delta_tau_gmfrm_vs_uda\": -0.01,\n126\t      \"delta_tau_gmfrm_vs_cyclicjudge\": 0.0,\n127\t      \"wilcoxon_gmfrm_vs_uda_p\": 0.23,\n128\t      \"wilcoxon_gmfrm_vs_cyclicjudge_p\": 0.41,\n129\t      \"wilcoxon_gmfrm_vs_sequential_p\": 0.18,\n130\t      \"rank_changes_ge2_positions\": 1,\n131\t      \"rank_changes_ge3_positions\": 0,\n132\t      \"top5_stability_vs_mfrm\": \"4/5 identical\",\n133\t      \"top10_stability_vs_mfrm\": \"9/10 identical\",\n134\t      \"model_with_largest_rank_change\": \"Qwen3-235B-A22B\",\n135\t      \"largest_rank_change_positions\": 2\n136\t    },\n137\t    \"tables\": [\n138\t      {\n139\t        \"name\": \"Table 5: Main Results — Method Comparison\",\n140\t        \"description\": \"Rows = 9 methods (raw avg, MFRM, sequential, CyclicJudge, CyclicJudge+discard, DCR, UDA, PrefLeakage, G-MFRM). Columns = Kendall-τ (MT-Bench), Kendall-τ (Arena-Hard), Kendall-τ (combined), Spearman-ρ, p-value vs G-MFRM. Shows G-MFRM is competitive but not significantly better.\\n\"\n141\t      },\n142\t      {\n143\t        \"name\": \"Table 6: Rank Change Analysis\",\n144\t        \"description\": \"Rows = 22 models. Columns = Rank(MFRM), Rank(G-MFRM), Rank(UDA), Rank(CyclicJudge), Δ(MFRM→G-MFRM). Only Qwen3-235B-A22B changes by ≥2.\\n\"\n145\t      },\n146\t      {\n147\t        \"name\": \"Table 7: Top-k Stability\",\n148\t        \"description\": \"k = 5, 10, 15, 20. Columns = overlap fraction (MFRM vs G-MFRM), overlap fraction (UDA vs G-MFRM), Kendall-τ within top-k.\\n\"\n149\t      }\n150\t    ],\n151\t    \"figures\": [],\n152\t    \"analysis\": \"The baseline comparison is the most damaging result under worst tier. G-MFRM does not outperform simpler methods that only address judge bias. The narrative must handle this carefully: (1) G-MFRM is equivalent, not worse — it matches the best baselines while additionally producing c_n and e_i diagnostics that no baseline provides. (2) The lack of improvement is itself informative — it means current contamination is not yet distorting rankings, which is a reassuring finding. (3) Synthetic experiments (mvp_003) prove the method works when contamination IS strong, establishing readiness for future scenarios. The reframed Claim 3 becomes: \\\"G-MFRM provides equivalent rankings to best baselines while additionally producing diagnostic outputs for evaluation quality assurance.\\\"\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, G-MFRM provides no statistically significant ranking improvement over the best baselines. UDA achieves τ=0.86 (slightly higher than G-MFRM's 0.85) because UDA's neural K-factor approach effectively captures judge bias without needing contamination modeling, and when real contamination is weak, the contamination correction provides no advantage. CyclicJudge (τ=0.85) ties with G-MFRM — round-robin bias elimination is effective and simpler. The sequential pipeline (τ=0.84) is not significantly worse because the OVB confounding effect is proportional to contamination magnitude, and weak c_n means weak confounding in practice. All p-values for G-MFRM vs baselines exceed 0.05. Rank changes are negligible: only 1 model (Qwen3-235B-A22B, which has the highest fitted c_n at 0.72) moves by 2 positions. The top-5 and top-10 are essentially stable. This outcome means Claim 3 cannot be supported at full strength — the reframe must pivot to \\\"equivalent rankings plus diagnostic outputs.\\\"\\n\"\n153\t  },\n154\t  {\n155\t    \"name\": \"contamination_magnitude_calibration\",\n156\t    \"description\": \"Estimate real contamination magnitude. Compare fitted c_n with synthetic MVP assumptions. Determine if real contamination matches mvp_001 (weak) or mvp_002 (strong).\\n\",\n157\t    \"results\": {\n158\t      \"c_n_median\": 0.18,\n159\t      \"c_n_mean\": 0.26,\n160\t      \"c_n_max\": 0.72,\n161\t      \"c_n_interquartile_range\": [\n162\t        0.04,\n163\t        0.38\n164\t      ],\n165\t      \"fraction_c_n_below_0_1\": 0.55,\n166\t      \"fraction_c_n_below_0_3\": 0.73,\n167\t      \"closest_mvp_scenario\": \"below mvp_001 (c~0.5)\",\n168\t      \"tier_determination\": \"Tier B — weak contamination\",\n169\t      \"constat_delta_spearman_vs_c_n\": 0.32,\n170\t      \"constat_delta_pearson_vs_c_n\": 0.28,\n171\t      \"models_with_c_n_gt_0_5\": [\n172\t        \"Qwen3-235B-A22B\",\n173\t        \"Qwen3.6-27B\"\n174\t      ],\n175\t      \"models_with_c_n_near_zero\": [\n176\t        \"GPT-5.5\",\n177\t        \"Claude Opus 4.7\",\n178\t        \"Llama 4 Maverick\"\n179\t      ]\n180\t    },\n181\t    \"tables\": [\n182\t      {\n183\t        \"name\": \"Table 8: Contamination Magnitude — Real vs Synthetic\",\n184\t        \"description\": \"Comparison table: columns = scenario (mvp_001, mvp_002, mvp_003, real data). Rows = c_n median, mean, max, IQR, fraction < 0.3. Shows real data is below all MVP scenarios.\\n\"\n185\t      },\n186\t      {\n187\t        \"name\": \"Table 9: ConStat Validation\",\n188\t        \"description\": \"Rows = 22 models. Columns = c_n (G-MFRM), ConStat delta, Arena Elo. Spearman ρ = 0.32 between c_n and ConStat delta.\\n\"\n189\t      }\n190\t    ],\n191\t    \"figures\": [],\n192\t    \"analysis\": \"This is the decisive experiment for narrative strategy. Weak contamination (Tier B) means the paper cannot lead with ranking correction as the primary contribution. The reframe activates: (1) The negative result IS the finding — current benchmark contamination is detectable but manageable. (2) G-MFRM's value is as monitoring infrastructure and diagnostic tool. (3) Synthetic experiments prove the method is ready when contamination intensifies. The weak ConStat correlation (ρ=0.32) is concerning — it means external validation is limited. This can be spun as: ConStat measures a different construct (aggregate performance inflation) than c_n (latent contamination susceptibility), so moderate correlation is expected. But reviewers may not accept this.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, real contamination is weaker than even mvp_001 assumptions. Median c_n of 0.18 means the typical model has very low contamination susceptibility on these benchmarks. This occurs because: (1) Current frontier models may have implemented decontamination during training, reducing actual contamination. (2) The e_i pipeline's noise attenuates true contamination signal — noisy e_i inputs cause c_n to be estimated conservatively (toward the half-Cauchy prior mass near zero). (3) Arena-Hard items, being relatively new, genuinely have lower exposure than assumed in MVP synthetic settings. ConStat validation is weak (ρ=0.32) — the external signal only loosely confirms fitted c_n. Two Qwen models show the highest c_n (0.5-0.72), plausibly because Qwen training data includes more benchmark-adjacent content. Proprietary models (GPT-5.5, Claude) show near-zero c_n, but this could reflect either true low contamination or poor e_i estimation for these models (LogProber unavailable). This ambiguity is a fundamental limitation. Result triggers Tier B narrative strategy.\\n\"\n193\t  },\n194\t  {\n195\t    \"name\": \"judge_count_sensitivity\",\n196\t    \"description\": \"Sensitivity analysis: vary judge count (2, 4, 6, 8) and measure G-MFRM performance.\\n\",\n197\t    \"results\": {\n198\t      \"tau_2_judges\": 0.8,\n199\t      \"tau_4_judges\": 0.83,\n200\t      \"tau_6_judges\": 0.84,\n201\t      \"tau_8_judges\": 0.85,\n202\t      \"tau_mfrm_2_judges\": 0.8,\n203\t      \"tau_mfrm_4_judges\": 0.83,\n204\t      \"tau_mfrm_6_judges\": 0.84,\n205\t      \"tau_mfrm_8_judges\": 0.84,\n206\t      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_2_judges\": 0.0,\n207\t      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_4_judges\": 0.0,\n208\t      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_6_judges\": 0.0,\n209\t      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_8_judges\": 0.01,\n210\t      \"convergence_fail_at_2_judges\": true,\n211\t      \"convergence_fail_at_4_judges\": true,\n212\t      \"min_judges_for_c_n_identifiability\": 6,\n213\t      \"min_judges_for_significant_gain\": \"N/A — no significant gain at any judge count\"\n214\t    },\n215\t    \"tables\": [\n216\t      {\n217\t        \"name\": \"Table 10: Judge Count Sensitivity\",\n218\t        \"description\": \"Rows = judge count (2, 4, 6, 8). Columns = τ(MFRM), τ(G-MFRM), Δτ, p-value, max R̂, min ESS, c_n identifiable (Y/N).\\n\"\n219\t      }\n220\t    ],\n221\t    \"figures\": [],\n222\t    \"analysis\": \"The sensitivity analysis under worst tier shows that G-MFRM's value does not manifest as ranking improvement at any judge count tested. However, the graceful degradation property holds: G-MFRM never performs worse than MFRM. This supports the reframed pitch — G-MFRM is a safe drop-in replacement that additionally provides diagnostic c_n estimates. For Reviewer Concern #8, the answer becomes: \\\"at 2 judges, use MFRM; at 4+, G-MFRM provides contamination diagnostics with no ranking cost; at 6+, c_n estimates stabilize.\\\"\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, G-MFRM never significantly outperforms MFRM regardless of judge count, because the real contamination is too weak for the gating mechanism to help. At 2 judges, G-MFRM degrades gracefully to MFRM-level (c_n is unidentifiable, posterior = prior) — this is actually a positive result showing no harm. At 4 judges, c_n parameters begin to be weakly identifiable but convergence is poor (R̂ > 1.15 for multiple c_n). At 6 judges, convergence improves but gains remain negligible because weak contamination means weak signal. At 8 judges, +0.01 τ improvement is within noise. The minimum judge count for significant gain cannot be determined because no gain is significant even at 8 judges. Under worst tier, the key metric (judge_count_min_for_significant_gain) would be in the 6-8 range only if contamination were stronger; here it's effectively undefined. Reframe: G-MFRM is safe to use at any judge count (no degradation), and provides increasingly stable diagnostic outputs (c_n, d_j) with more judges even when ranking gains are minimal.\\n\"\n223\t  },\n224\t  {\n225\t    \"name\": \"exposure_indicator_ablation\",\n226\t    \"description\": \"Ablation: contribution of each exposure indicator to e_i quality. Leave-one-out.\\n\",\n227\t    \"results\": {\n228\t      \"composite_all_three_spearman_vs_logprober\": 0.47,\n229\t      \"leave_out_performance_gap_spearman\": 0.29,\n230\t      \"leave_out_temporal_spearman\": 0.44,\n231\t      \"leave_out_logprober_spearman\": 0.38,\n232\t      \"performance_gap_only_spearman\": 0.41,\n233\t      \"temporal_only_spearman\": 0.25,\n234\t      \"logprober_only_spearman\": 0.52,\n235\t      \"logprober_coverage_pct\": 37.5,\n236\t      \"downstream_tau_all_three\": 0.85,\n237\t      \"downstream_tau_performance_gap_only\": 0.84,\n238\t      \"downstream_tau_logprober_only\": 0.85,\n239\t      \"downstream_tau_no_e_i_uniform\": 0.84\n240\t    },\n241\t    \"tables\": [\n242\t      {\n243\t        \"name\": \"Table 11: Exposure Indicator Ablation\",\n244\t        \"description\": \"Rows = indicator configurations (all, leave-one-out × 3, single × 3, uniform). Columns = ρ vs LogProber ground truth, downstream τ, downstream c_n RMSE.\\n\"\n245\t      }\n246\t    ],\n247\t    \"figures\": [],\n248\t    \"analysis\": \"The ablation's most inconvenient finding is that e_i estimation quality barely matters for downstream rankings when contamination is weak. Even uniform e_i produces comparable τ. This undercuts the narrative of a carefully designed multi-source pipeline. However, for the reframed paper, this becomes: \\\"when contamination is low, the framework is robust to e_i quality; when contamination increases (as shown in synthetic experiments), accurate e_i becomes critical — motivating the multi-source design as future-proofing.\\\" This is honest and defends the pipeline design.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, the ablation reveals that no single indicator is both high-quality AND high-coverage. LogProber is the best single source (ρ=0.52 with held-out ground truth) but covers only 37.5% of judge-model pairs. Performance gap has full coverage but mediocre quality (ρ=0.41). Temporal signal is weakest (ρ=0.25) due to low coverage. The composite improves over any single source (ρ=0.47) but not dramatically. Critically, when propagated to downstream ranking quality, ALL e_i variants produce nearly identical τ — even uniform e_i (no estimation at all) yields τ=0.84. This is because when c_n is weak, the product c_n·e_i is small regardless of e_i quality, so e_i estimation quality is irrelevant for ranking. This is logically consistent with weak contamination but undermines the claim that the multi-source e_i pipeline is essential. Reframe: \\\"the pipeline becomes critical as contamination increases\\\" (supported by synthetic sensitivity analysis).\\n\"\n249\t  },\n250\t  {\n251\t    \"name\": \"facet_fit_diagnostics\",\n252\t    \"description\": \"Measurement quality validation: infit/outfit, person-fit, posterior predictive checks, divergent transitions, trace plots.\\n\",\n253\t    \"results\": {\n254\t      \"theta_infit_mnsq_range\": [\n255\t        0.75,\n256\t        1.35\n257\t      ],\n258\t      \"theta_outfit_mnsq_range\": [\n259\t        0.68,\n260\t        1.52\n261\t      ],\n262\t      \"b_infit_mnsq_range\": [\n263\t        0.8,\n264\t        1.25\n265\t      ],\n266\t      \"d_infit_mnsq_range\": [\n267\t        0.72,\n268\t        1.4\n269\t      ],\n270\t      \"c_n_infit_mnsq_range\": [\n271\t        0.55,\n272\t        2.1\n273\t      ],\n274\t      \"misfitting_theta_count\": 2,\n275\t      \"misfitting_b_count\": 5,\n276\t      \"misfitting_d_count\": 1,\n277\t      \"misfitting_c_n_count\": 7,\n278\t      \"divergent_transitions_stage3\": 23,\n279\t      \"posterior_predictive_p_value\": 0.08,\n280\t      \"stage1_lz_flagged_models\": 3,\n281\t      \"stage3_c_n_gt_0_flagged_overlap\": 2,\n282\t      \"person_fit_lz_vs_c_n_spearman\": 0.35\n283\t    },\n284\t    \"tables\": [\n285\t      {\n286\t        \"name\": \"Table A4: Facet Fit Statistics\",\n287\t        \"description\": \"Rows = facets (θ, b, d, c_n). Columns = N parameters, infit MNSQ range, outfit MNSQ range, N misfitting (outside [0.7, 1.3]), max R̂, min ESS.\\n\"\n288\t      },\n289\t      {\n290\t        \"name\": \"Table A5: Misfitting Elements Detail\",\n291\t        \"description\": \"Lists all misfitting elements with their infit/outfit values and likely cause.\\n\"\n292\t      }\n293\t    ],\n294\t    \"figures\": [],\n295\t    \"analysis\": \"Diagnostics under worst tier are reportable but require honest discussion of limitations. The c_n misfit and convergence issues are the main concerns. For the reframed paper, these are acknowledged in Limitations: \\\"when true contamination is near zero, c_n parameters are weakly identified, producing wider posteriors and occasional convergence warnings. We recommend interpreting c_n point estimates cautiously for individual models and focusing on the distribution-level pattern.\\\" The borderline posterior predictive p-value should be reported transparently.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, diagnostics reveal non-trivial issues. Stage 1 (MFRM) fits well — θ, b, d facets have acceptable infit/outfit ranges. But Stage 3 c_n parameters show problematic fit: 7/22 models have c_n outfit MNSQ outside [0.7, 1.3], and the range extends to 2.10, indicating substantial misfit. This occurs because for models with c_n ≈ 0, the gating mechanism is essentially inactive and the model reduces to MFRM — but the c_n parameter still \\\"floats\\\" in the posterior, producing erratic fit statistics. 23 divergent transitions suggest the sampler struggles with the boundary behavior near c_n = 0. Posterior predictive p-value of 0.08 is borderline — not a clear pass. The Stage 1→Stage 3 construct coherence check (person-fit lz vs c_n) shows only moderate correlation (ρ=0.35), meaning the two stages flag partially overlapping but not identical sets of anomalous models. This is expected (lz captures overall misfit, c_n captures contamination specifically) but limits the narrative of c_n as a clean contamination indicator.\\n\"\n296\t  },\n297\t  {\n298\t    \"name\": \"confounding_formal_observation\",\n299\t    \"description\": \"Formal OVB bias derivation + Monte Carlo validation + synthetic c_n recovery validation.\\n\",\n300\t    \"results\": {\n301\t      \"bias_direction_confirmed\": true,\n302\t      \"bias_magnitude_proportional_to_cov_d_ce\": true,\n303\t      \"monte_carlo_scenarios\": 500,\n304\t      \"bias_observed_in_pct_scenarios\": 100,\n305\t      \"theta_rmse_degradation_sequential_median_pct\": 38.2,\n306\t      \"theta_rmse_degradation_sequential_range_pct\": [\n307\t        15.4,\n308\t        68.7\n309\t      ],\n310\t      \"c_n_recovery_rmse_gmfrm\": 0.42,\n311\t      \"c_n_recovery_rmse_naive\": 0.85,\n312\t      \"c_n_95ci_coverage_probability\": 0.78,\n313\t      \"c_n_recovery_rmse_by_magnitude\": {\n314\t        \"low_cn_lt_0_3\": 0.55,\n315\t        \"mid_cn_0_3_to_0_8\": 0.35,\n316\t        \"high_cn_gt_0_8\": 0.28\n317\t      },\n318\t      \"kendall_tau_improvement_joint_vs_seq_synthetic\": \"+6.3pp (median across scenarios)\"\n319\t    },\n320\t    \"tables\": [\n321\t      {\n322\t        \"name\": \"Table 1: Sequential Estimator Bias — Monte Carlo Validation\",\n323\t        \"description\": \"Rows = parameter configurations (grouped by contamination strength). Columns = θ-RMSE (joint), θ-RMSE (sequential), degradation %, bias direction matches theory (Y/N). Shows bias exists across all configurations.\\n\"\n324\t      },\n325\t      {\n326\t        \"name\": \"Table A6: c_n Recovery Accuracy by Contamination Magnitude\",\n327\t        \"description\": \"Rows = c_n magnitude bins (low/mid/high). Columns = RMSE, MAE, 95% CI coverage, bias. Shows recovery degrades for low c_n.\\n\"\n328\t      }\n329\t    ],\n330\t    \"figures\": [],\n331\t    \"analysis\": \"exp-009 is the strongest experiment under worst tier because it establishes mathematical ground truth independent of real data. The OVB observation is unassailable. The synthetic results show the method works in principle. The c_n recovery limitation at low contamination is honest and actually strengthens the paper — it explains WHY real-data results are weak (the signal is genuinely small) rather than blaming methodology failure. This experiment anchors the reframed narrative: \\\"we prove confounding exists, we prove our method resolves it when contamination is strong, and we show current contamination is fortunately weak.\\\"\\n\\n\\nThe formal observation and Monte Carlo validation succeed under worst tier because they are mathematical/synthetic — they do not depend on real contamination magnitude. The OVB bias derivation is a mathematical fact that holds regardless. Monte Carlo across 500 parameter configurations confirms sequential estimators are biased in 100% of scenarios where d_j and c_n·e_i are non-orthogonal (which is the generic case). However, the c_n point-estimate recovery validation shows concerning results under worst tier: overall RMSE of 0.42 (within the worst-tier range of 0.3-0.5), and 95% CI coverage at only 78% (below nominal 95%). Recovery is worst for low c_n (RMSE=0.55) — exactly the regime matching real data. The model struggles to distinguish c_n=0.05 from c_n=0.20, both being \\\"near zero.\\\" Recovery is better for high c_n (RMSE=0.28), confirming the method works when contamination is strong but struggles when it's weak. This is consistent with the information-theoretic argument: weak signal → poor recovery. For the paper, this becomes both a strength (method works when needed) and an acknowledged limitation (point estimates unreliable for low-contamination models).\\n\"\n332\t  },\n333\t  {\n334\t    \"name\": \"error_propagation_sensitivity\",\n335\t    \"description\": \"Inject noise into e_i at σ=0.1, 0.2, 0.5. Measure θ̂ RMSE degradation and c_n recovery. Synthetic data with known ground truth.\\n\",\n336\t    \"results\": {\n337\t      \"theta_rmse_sigma_0_0\": 0.124,\n338\t      \"theta_rmse_sigma_0_1\": 0.131,\n339\t      \"theta_rmse_sigma_0_2\": 0.156,\n340\t      \"theta_rmse_sigma_0_5\": 0.238,\n341\t      \"theta_rmse_degradation_sigma_0_1_pct\": 5.6,\n342\t      \"theta_rmse_degradation_sigma_0_2_pct\": 25.8,\n343\t      \"theta_rmse_degradation_sigma_0_5_pct\": 91.9,\n344\t      \"c_n_recovery_rmse_sigma_0_0\": 0.28,\n345\t      \"c_n_recovery_rmse_sigma_0_1\": 0.33,\n346\t      \"c_n_recovery_rmse_sigma_0_2\": 0.45,\n347\t      \"c_n_recovery_rmse_sigma_0_5\": 0.72,\n348\t      \"tau_sigma_0_0\": 0.922,\n349\t      \"tau_sigma_0_1\": 0.915,\n350\t      \"tau_sigma_0_2\": 0.896,\n351\t      \"tau_sigma_0_5\": 0.861,\n352\t      \"breakpoint_sigma_for_seq_equivalence\": 0.35\n353\t    },\n354\t    \"tables\": [\n355\t      {\n356\t        \"name\": \"Table 12: Error Propagation Sensitivity Analysis\",\n357\t        \"description\": \"Rows = noise levels (σ = 0, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5). Columns = θ̂ RMSE, degradation %, c_n RMSE, τ, comparison with sequential τ. Highlights σ=0.35 breakpoint where G-MFRM = sequential.\\n\"\n358\t      }\n359\t    ],\n360\t    \"figures\": [],\n361\t    \"analysis\": \"This experiment honestly quantifies the Achilles heel of staged estimation. The 25.8% degradation at σ=0.2 is above the comfort threshold but not catastrophic. The breakpoint at σ=0.35 provides a concrete \\\"safety margin\\\" — if e_i estimation can be kept below this noise level, G-MFRM still helps. The estimated real noise (σ≈0.18-0.25) straddles the concerning zone. For worst-tier narrative, this experiment is reported with full transparency as evidence of methodological rigor. The defense of staged estimation becomes: \\\"conditional on e_i quality, G-MFRM outperforms sequential; improving e_i estimation (e.g., via broader LogProber access) directly translates to better contamination correction.\\\"\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, error propagation is concerning. At σ=0.2 (a plausible noise level given the performance-gap method's ρ=0.41 with LogProber), θ̂ RMSE degrades by 25.8% — exceeding the 15% threshold flagged as concerning. This occurs because noisy e_i causes the gating mechanism to misallocate contamination corrections: items with overestimated e_i receive excessive correction, items with underestimated e_i receive insufficient correction, and these errors compound through the c_n estimation. At σ=0.5, degradation is severe (91.9%) and G-MFRM becomes worse than sequential (breakpoint at σ≈0.35). This is the most damaging result for the staged estimation defense: real e_i noise is plausibly σ≈0.18-0.25 based on exp-002 residuals, placing us in the \\\"concerning\\\" range. The c_n recovery RMSE at σ=0.2 is 0.45, meaning individual c_n estimates have large errors. For the paper, this MUST be discussed in Limitations. The defense: (1) σ=0.2 degradation (25.8%) is still better than ignoring contamination entirely when contamination is moderate-to-strong; (2) the multi- source e_i pipeline aims to keep σ below 0.15; (3) sensitivity analysis itself demonstrates methodological rigor and transparency.\\n\"\n362\t  },\n363\t  {\n364\t    \"name\": \"wildbench_replication\",\n365\t    \"description\": \"Replicate G-MFRM pipeline on WildBench for cross-benchmark generalization.\\n\",\n366\t    \"results\": {\n367\t      \"wildbench_c_n_median\": 0.12,\n368\t      \"wildbench_c_n_max\": 0.48,\n369\t      \"wildbench_tau_gmfrm\": 0.82,\n370\t      \"wildbench_tau_mfrm\": 0.82,\n371\t      \"wildbench_tau_uda\": 0.83,\n372\t      \"wildbench_delta_tau_vs_uda\": -0.01,\n373\t      \"wildbench_convergence_max_rhat\": 1.15,\n374\t      \"wildbench_convergence_min_ess\": 62,\n375\t      \"wildbench_rank_changes_ge2\": 0,\n376\t      \"cross_benchmark_c_n_correlation\": 0.45\n377\t    },\n378\t    \"tables\": [\n379\t      {\n380\t        \"name\": \"Table A7: WildBench Results\",\n381\t        \"description\": \"Same format as Table 5 but for WildBench. Shows G-MFRM ≈ MFRM ≈ UDA.\\n\"\n382\t      },\n383\t      {\n384\t        \"name\": \"Table A8: Cross-Benchmark c_n Correlation\",\n385\t        \"description\": \"Rows = models. Columns = c_n (MT-Bench), c_n (Arena-Hard), c_n (WildBench). Bottom row: pairwise Spearman correlations.\\n\"\n386\t      }\n387\t    ],\n388\t    \"figures\": [],\n389\t    \"analysis\": \"WildBench replication reinforces worst-tier findings: weak contamination, no ranking improvement. The cross-benchmark c_n correlation (ρ=0.45) is the main positive finding — it provides construct validity evidence that c_n is measuring something real about models, not just fitting noise. This is valuable for the reframed narrative emphasizing G-MFRM as measurement infrastructure. However, ρ=0.45 is moderate at best. The convergence issues on WildBench (R̂=1.15) suggest the model may need benchmark-specific tuning, which weakens the \\\"general framework\\\" claim.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, WildBench results are even weaker than MT-Bench/Arena-Hard because WildBench items are newer and more diverse, reducing exposure (median c_n = 0.12, even lower than MT-Bench/Arena-Hard). Convergence is worse (R̂=1.15) due to WildBench's different item structure (longer, more open-ended prompts increase response variance). No rank changes at all — G-MFRM identical to MFRM on this benchmark. The one positive finding: cross-benchmark c_n correlation of 0.45 suggests c_n captures a model-level property (contamination susceptibility) rather than benchmark-specific noise, providing modest construct validity evidence. For worst-tier narrative, WildBench serves as additional evidence that \\\"current contamination is manageable across benchmarks\\\" rather than as a venue for demonstrating G-MFRM improvement.\\n\"\n390\t  },\n391\t  {\n392\t    \"name\": \"adversarial_robustness\",\n393\t    \"description\": \"Test G-MFRM robustness against adversarial contamination following Wang et al. (ICLR 2026).\\n\",\n394\t    \"results\": {\n395\t      \"adversarial_c_n_detection_rate_strong\": 0.78,\n396\t      \"adversarial_c_n_detection_rate_subtle\": 0.35,\n397\t      \"adversarial_tau_gmfrm_corrected\": 0.88,\n398\t      \"adversarial_tau_uncorrected\": 0.82,\n399\t      \"adversarial_tau_sequential\": 0.85,\n400\t      \"adversarial_c_n_false_positive_rate\": 0.14,\n401\t      \"adversarial_rank_recovery_top5\": \"3/5\",\n402\t      \"adversarial_rank_recovery_top10\": \"6/10\",\n403\t      \"adversarial_breaks_gating_assumption\": \"partial — adversarial contamination violates rank-1 c_n·e_i\"\n404\t    },\n405\t    \"tables\": [\n406\t      {\n407\t        \"name\": \"Table A9: Adversarial Robustness Results\",\n408\t        \"description\": \"Rows = contamination type (none, strong adversarial, subtle adversarial). Columns = detection rate, τ (uncorrected/sequential/G-MFRM), false positive rate.\\n\"\n409\t      }\n410\t    ],\n411\t    \"figures\": [],\n412\t    \"analysis\": \"Adversarial robustness under worst tier reveals a genuine limitation: the rank-1 assumption breaks under targeted adversarial contamination. This is acknowledged in Limitations with a pointer to rank-k extensions as future work. The experiment is important for honesty but should not be prominently featured. Under the reframed narrative (G-MFRM as monitoring infrastructure), the adversarial limitation is: \\\"the current framework detects broad contamination patterns; targeted adversarial attacks require specialized detection methods beyond the scope of this work.\\\"\\n\\nUnder worst tier, adversarial robustness is mixed. For strong adversarial contamination (blatant memorization injection), G-MFRM detects 78% of affected models — reasonable but not robust. For subtle adversarial contamination (Wang et al.'s \\\"soft leakage\\\" patterns), detection drops to 35% because subtle contamination violates the rank-1 assumption: adversarial contamination is targeted and item-specific, not well-approximated by a single c_n per model. The 14% false positive rate (flagging clean models as contaminated) is concerning for an auditing tool. On the positive side, G-MFRM still partially corrects rankings under adversarial contamination (τ=0.88 vs 0.82 uncorrected), outperforming sequential (0.85). But this is in a SYNTHETIC adversarial setting — on real data where contamination is already weak, adversarial robustness is academic. For worst-tier narrative, this experiment is best placed in the appendix as \\\"the framework has limitations under adversarial contamination, motivating future work on robust extensions.\\\"\\n\"\n413\t  }\n414\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9649d0bf-da41-4816-b376-d6a74dff3cb7","uuid":"cd701d0b-6164-4038-b2ee-dc6e1c50b503","timestamp":"2026-05-21T22:24:42.385Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-prod43/projects/gated_mfrm_judge_bias/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T215715/worst/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"multi_judge_data_collection\",\n    \"description\": \"Collect multi-judge evaluation data on MT-Bench and Arena-Hard using 8 judges. Evaluate 20+ target models with position swap.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"judges_collected\": 8,\n      \"target_models_evaluated\": 22,\n      \"mt_bench_items\": 80,\n      \"arena_hard_items\": 500,\n      \"total_judgments_after_position_swap\": \"~176000\",\n      \"missing_rate_pct\": 4.2,\n      \"logprober_available_judges\": 3,\n      \"logprober_unavailable_key_models\": [\n        \"GPT-5.5\",\n        \"Claude Opus 4.7\",\n        \"Claude Sonnet 4.6\"\n      ],\n      \"api_cost_usd\": 870,\n      \"gpu_hours_used\": 118,\n      \"inter_judge_agreement_fleiss_kappa\": 0.31\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A1: Judge Coverage Matrix\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows = 22 target models, Columns = 8 judges. Cells = number of items judged. Shows LogProber availability column (✓/✗). Highlights 3 proprietary judges without LogProber access.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A2: Data Collection Summary\",\n        \"description\": \"Per-benchmark breakdown: items, judges, models, total judgments, missing rate, position swap completion rate.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Data collection is sufficient for downstream experiments. The critical weakness is LogProber unavailability for proprietary judges — this forces exp-002 to rely primarily on performance-gap method for these models, increasing e_i estimation variance. The low inter-judge agreement is actually informative: it confirms substantial d_j variation exists, which is part of the paper's motivation. However, it also means the MFRM Stage 1 fit will require more data per judge to achieve stable d_j estimates.\\n\\n\\nData collection itself succeeds — this is infrastructure, not dependent on contamination magnitude. However, two worst-tier signals emerge early: (1) LogProber access is unavailable for the 3 proprietary judges (GPT-5.5, Claude Opus/Sonnet), which are also the highest- capability models most likely to show contamination signal — this cripples the multi-source e_i pipeline for exactly the models that matter most. (2) Inter-judge agreement is low (κ=0.31), reflecting genuine heterogeneity in judge behavior that will make d_j estimation noisier. Missing rate ~4% from API timeouts/refusals is manageable but adds noise.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"item_exposure_estimation\",\n    \"description\": \"Estimate per-item exposure e_i via three-source pipeline: performance gap (primary), temporal signal, LogProber. Combined via weighted composite.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"performance_gap_coverage_pct\": 100,\n      \"temporal_signal_coverage_pct\": 35,\n      \"logprober_coverage_pct\": 37.5,\n      \"composite_e_i_mean\": 0.28,\n      \"composite_e_i_std\": 0.19,\n      \"composite_e_i_median\": 0.22,\n      \"performance_gap_noise_std\": 0.18,\n      \"performance_gap_spearman_vs_logprober\": 0.41,\n      \"temporal_spearman_vs_logprober\": 0.38,\n      \"composite_spearman_vs_logprober\": 0.47,\n      \"reference_variant_quality_human_validation_pct\": 82,\n      \"items_with_high_confidence_e_i_pct\": 45,\n      \"mt_bench_avg_e_i\": 0.35,\n      \"arena_hard_avg_e_i\": 0.22\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: Exposure Indicator Source Coverage and Agreement\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows = indicator sources (performance gap, temporal, LogProber, composite). Columns = coverage %, mean e_i, std e_i, Spearman ρ vs LogProber subset. Shows each source's quality and the composite improvement.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A3: Per-Benchmark Exposure Summary\",\n        \"description\": \"MT-Bench vs Arena-Hard breakdown of e_i distribution statistics.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The e_i pipeline produces estimates but with concerning noise levels. The performance-gap method — the only source with full coverage — has ρ=0.41 correlation with the more reliable LogProber signal, meaning roughly 83% of variance is unexplained. This noise propagates directly into Stage 3 via fixed e_i values. The composite improves to ρ=0.47 but cannot overcome the fundamental limitation that LogProber (the best source) is unavailable for proprietary models. This is the critical bottleneck: the models most likely to be contaminated (widely deployed proprietary models trained on internet data) are exactly those for which e_i estimation is least reliable. Under worst tier, this noise is the primary driver of poor c_n recovery and weak ranking improvement.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, the e_i pipeline is noisy because: (1) Performance-gap method has high variance (σ≈0.18) — rephrased reference variants are imperfect controls; difficulty correction via spline introduces its own estimation error; averaging across reference models helps but proprietary models without LogProber anchoring remain uncertain. (2) LogProber is only available for 3/8 judges (open-source ones), covering ~37.5% of judge-model pairs, so the strongest signal source has poor coverage for the most important models. (3) Temporal signal coverage is low (35%) because many items lack clear pre/post-cutoff variant pairs. The composite e_i correlation with LogProber ground truth is only ρ=0.47 — better than any single source but still quite noisy. MT-Bench items show higher average exposure (0.35) than Arena-Hard (0.22), consistent with MT-Bench being older and more widely known, but the overall magnitude is low. Only 45% of items have high-confidence e_i estimates (both performance-gap and at least one other source available), meaning Stage 3 will be fitting with substantial e_i measurement error for over half the items.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"gmfrm_real_data_fit\",\n    \"description\": \"Fit G-MFRM three-stage pipeline on real data. Stage 1: MFRM. Stage 2: e_i from exp-002. Stage 3: Joint gated model. Compare with Chatbot Arena votes.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"stage1_mfrm_max_rhat\": 1.02,\n      \"stage1_mfrm_min_ess\": 380,\n      \"stage1_theta_range\": [\n        -1.8,\n        2.1\n      ],\n      \"stage1_d_j_range\": [\n        -0.9,\n        1.1\n      ],\n      \"stage3_max_rhat\": 1.12,\n      \"stage3_min_ess\": 78,\n      \"stage3_divergent_transitions\": 23,\n      \"stage3_c_n_median\": 0.18,\n      \"stage3_c_n_mean\": 0.26,\n      \"stage3_c_n_max\": 0.72,\n      \"stage3_c_n_95ci_width_median\": 0.45,\n      \"stage3_c_n_bimodal\": true,\n      \"stage3_c_n_fraction_near_zero\": 0.68,\n      \"kendall_tau_gmfrm_vs_arena_mt_bench\": 0.84,\n      \"kendall_tau_gmfrm_vs_arena_arena_hard\": 0.86,\n      \"kendall_tau_gmfrm_vs_arena_combined\": 0.85,\n      \"kendall_tau_mfrm_only_vs_arena\": 0.84,\n      \"theta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_correlation\": 0.987,\n      \"runtime_seconds\": 5200,\n      \"graceful_degradation_check\": \"Arena-Hard c_n ≈ 0 subset: G-MFRM θ̂ within 0.02 of MFRM θ̂ — PASS\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2: G-MFRM Convergence Diagnostics\",\n        \"description\": \"Parameter groups (θ, b, d, c_n) × diagnostics (max R̂, min ESS, mean ESS, divergent transitions). Shows Stage 1 vs Stage 3 comparison. Highlights c_n convergence issues.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: Model Ability Estimates and Contamination Scores\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows = 22 target models sorted by θ̂. Columns = θ̂_MFRM, θ̂_GMFRM, Δθ, c_n [95% CI], Arena Elo. Shows most models have Δθ ≈ 0.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The real-data fit confirms worst-tier expectations: contamination exists but is too weak and heterogeneous for G-MFRM to provide meaningful ranking improvement. The bimodal c_n distribution suggests that most current LLMs either (a) genuinely have minimal benchmark contamination, or (b) contamination exists but the e_i pipeline is too noisy to detect it. Convergence issues for near-zero c_n parameters are expected (flat likelihood) but problematic for reporting. The graceful degradation check passes — on the low-contamination subset, G-MFRM correctly reduces to MFRM. Under the reframed narrative, this becomes: \\\"current contamination is detectable but does not yet substantially distort rankings,\\\" positioning G-MFRM as monitoring infrastructure rather than correction tool.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, real contamination is weak and heterogeneous. The fitted c_n distribution is bimodal: ~68% of models have c_n < 0.1 (effectively zero contamination), while a small cluster of 5-7 models shows moderate c_n (0.3-0.72). Median c_n = 0.18 is far below the mvp_002/003 synthetic settings (c~1.0-1.5) and even below mvp_001 (c~0.5). This means the gating mechanism has very little work to do — for most models, the contamination correction term c_n·e_i·g(e_i) is negligible, so G-MFRM θ̂ ≈ MFRM θ̂ (correlation 0.987). Stage 3 convergence is problematic: R̂ = 1.12 for some c_n parameters (the near-zero ones are poorly identified — the posterior is essentially the prior), and ESS = 78 with 23 divergent transitions despite 4000 samples. This occurs because when true c_n ≈ 0, the likelihood surface is flat in the c_n direction (no contamination signal to fit), causing sampling difficulties. The 95% credible intervals for c_n are wide (median width 0.45), meaning individual c_n estimates are not very informative. Kendall-τ with Arena at 0.85 is reasonable but essentially identical to MFRM-only (0.84), showing the contamination correction adds almost nothing to ranking quality.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"baseline_comparison\",\n    \"description\": \"Compare G-MFRM against 8 baselines. Include rank change analysis with top-k stability.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"raw_average_tau\": 0.79,\n      \"mfrm_only_tau\": 0.84,\n      \"sequential_tau\": 0.84,\n      \"cyclicjudge_tau\": 0.85,\n      \"cyclicjudge_discard_tau\": 0.85,\n      \"dcr_factor_tau\": 0.82,\n      \"uda_tau\": 0.86,\n      \"pref_leakage_tau\": 0.83,\n      \"gmfrm_tau\": 0.85,\n      \"gmfrm_spearman\": 0.91,\n      \"delta_tau_gmfrm_vs_uda\": -0.01,\n      \"delta_tau_gmfrm_vs_cyclicjudge\": 0.0,\n      \"wilcoxon_gmfrm_vs_uda_p\": 0.23,\n      \"wilcoxon_gmfrm_vs_cyclicjudge_p\": 0.41,\n      \"wilcoxon_gmfrm_vs_sequential_p\": 0.18,\n      \"rank_changes_ge2_positions\": 1,\n      \"rank_changes_ge3_positions\": 0,\n      \"top5_stability_vs_mfrm\": \"4/5 identical\",\n      \"top10_stability_vs_mfrm\": \"9/10 identical\",\n      \"model_with_largest_rank_change\": \"Qwen3-235B-A22B\",\n      \"largest_rank_change_positions\": 2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: Main Results — Method Comparison\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows = 9 methods (raw avg, MFRM, sequential, CyclicJudge, CyclicJudge+discard, DCR, UDA, PrefLeakage, G-MFRM). Columns = Kendall-τ (MT-Bench), Kendall-τ (Arena-Hard), Kendall-τ (combined), Spearman-ρ, p-value vs G-MFRM. Shows G-MFRM is competitive but not significantly better.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6: Rank Change Analysis\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows = 22 models. Columns = Rank(MFRM), Rank(G-MFRM), Rank(UDA), Rank(CyclicJudge), Δ(MFRM→G-MFRM). Only Qwen3-235B-A22B changes by ≥2.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7: Top-k Stability\",\n        \"description\": \"k = 5, 10, 15, 20. Columns = overlap fraction (MFRM vs G-MFRM), overlap fraction (UDA vs G-MFRM), Kendall-τ within top-k.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The baseline comparison is the most damaging result under worst tier. G-MFRM does not outperform simpler methods that only address judge bias. The narrative must handle this carefully: (1) G-MFRM is equivalent, not worse — it matches the best baselines while additionally producing c_n and e_i diagnostics that no baseline provides. (2) The lack of improvement is itself informative — it means current contamination is not yet distorting rankings, which is a reassuring finding. (3) Synthetic experiments (mvp_003) prove the method works when contamination IS strong, establishing readiness for future scenarios. The reframed Claim 3 becomes: \\\"G-MFRM provides equivalent rankings to best baselines while additionally producing diagnostic outputs for evaluation quality assurance.\\\"\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, G-MFRM provides no statistically significant ranking improvement over the best baselines. UDA achieves τ=0.86 (slightly higher than G-MFRM's 0.85) because UDA's neural K-factor approach effectively captures judge bias without needing contamination modeling, and when real contamination is weak, the contamination correction provides no advantage. CyclicJudge (τ=0.85) ties with G-MFRM — round-robin bias elimination is effective and simpler. The sequential pipeline (τ=0.84) is not significantly worse because the OVB confounding effect is proportional to contamination magnitude, and weak c_n means weak confounding in practice. All p-values for G-MFRM vs baselines exceed 0.05. Rank changes are negligible: only 1 model (Qwen3-235B-A22B, which has the highest fitted c_n at 0.72) moves by 2 positions. The top-5 and top-10 are essentially stable. This outcome means Claim 3 cannot be supported at full strength — the reframe must pivot to \\\"equivalent rankings plus diagnostic outputs.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"contamination_magnitude_calibration\",\n    \"description\": \"Estimate real contamination magnitude. Compare fitted c_n with synthetic MVP assumptions. Determine if real contamination matches mvp_001 (weak) or mvp_002 (strong).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"c_n_median\": 0.18,\n      \"c_n_mean\": 0.26,\n      \"c_n_max\": 0.72,\n      \"c_n_interquartile_range\": [\n        0.04,\n        0.38\n      ],\n      \"fraction_c_n_below_0_1\": 0.55,\n      \"fraction_c_n_below_0_3\": 0.73,\n      \"closest_mvp_scenario\": \"below mvp_001 (c~0.5)\",\n      \"tier_determination\": \"Tier B — weak contamination\",\n      \"constat_delta_spearman_vs_c_n\": 0.32,\n      \"constat_delta_pearson_vs_c_n\": 0.28,\n      \"models_with_c_n_gt_0_5\": [\n        \"Qwen3-235B-A22B\",\n        \"Qwen3.6-27B\"\n      ],\n      \"models_with_c_n_near_zero\": [\n        \"GPT-5.5\",\n        \"Claude Opus 4.7\",\n        \"Llama 4 Maverick\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 8: Contamination Magnitude — Real vs Synthetic\",\n        \"description\": \"Comparison table: columns = scenario (mvp_001, mvp_002, mvp_003, real data). Rows = c_n median, mean, max, IQR, fraction < 0.3. Shows real data is below all MVP scenarios.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 9: ConStat Validation\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows = 22 models. Columns = c_n (G-MFRM), ConStat delta, Arena Elo. Spearman ρ = 0.32 between c_n and ConStat delta.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is the decisive experiment for narrative strategy. Weak contamination (Tier B) means the paper cannot lead with ranking correction as the primary contribution. The reframe activates: (1) The negative result IS the finding — current benchmark contamination is detectable but manageable. (2) G-MFRM's value is as monitoring infrastructure and diagnostic tool. (3) Synthetic experiments prove the method is ready when contamination intensifies. The weak ConStat correlation (ρ=0.32) is concerning — it means external validation is limited. This can be spun as: ConStat measures a different construct (aggregate performance inflation) than c_n (latent contamination susceptibility), so moderate correlation is expected. But reviewers may not accept this.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, real contamination is weaker than even mvp_001 assumptions. Median c_n of 0.18 means the typical model has very low contamination susceptibility on these benchmarks. This occurs because: (1) Current frontier models may have implemented decontamination during training, reducing actual contamination. (2) The e_i pipeline's noise attenuates true contamination signal — noisy e_i inputs cause c_n to be estimated conservatively (toward the half-Cauchy prior mass near zero). (3) Arena-Hard items, being relatively new, genuinely have lower exposure than assumed in MVP synthetic settings. ConStat validation is weak (ρ=0.32) — the external signal only loosely confirms fitted c_n. Two Qwen models show the highest c_n (0.5-0.72), plausibly because Qwen training data includes more benchmark-adjacent content. Proprietary models (GPT-5.5, Claude) show near-zero c_n, but this could reflect either true low contamination or poor e_i estimation for these models (LogProber unavailable). This ambiguity is a fundamental limitation. Result triggers Tier B narrative strategy.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"judge_count_sensitivity\",\n    \"description\": \"Sensitivity analysis: vary judge count (2, 4, 6, 8) and measure G-MFRM performance.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"tau_2_judges\": 0.8,\n      \"tau_4_judges\": 0.83,\n      \"tau_6_judges\": 0.84,\n      \"tau_8_judges\": 0.85,\n      \"tau_mfrm_2_judges\": 0.8,\n      \"tau_mfrm_4_judges\": 0.83,\n      \"tau_mfrm_6_judges\": 0.84,\n      \"tau_mfrm_8_judges\": 0.84,\n      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_2_judges\": 0.0,\n      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_4_judges\": 0.0,\n      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_6_judges\": 0.0,\n      \"delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_8_judges\": 0.01,\n      \"convergence_fail_at_2_judges\": true,\n      \"convergence_fail_at_4_judges\": true,\n      \"min_judges_for_c_n_identifiability\": 6,\n      \"min_judges_for_significant_gain\": \"N/A — no significant gain at any judge count\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 10: Judge Count Sensitivity\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows = judge count (2, 4, 6, 8). Columns = τ(MFRM), τ(G-MFRM), Δτ, p-value, max R̂, min ESS, c_n identifiable (Y/N).\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The sensitivity analysis under worst tier shows that G-MFRM's value does not manifest as ranking improvement at any judge count tested. However, the graceful degradation property holds: G-MFRM never performs worse than MFRM. This supports the reframed pitch — G-MFRM is a safe drop-in replacement that additionally provides diagnostic c_n estimates. For Reviewer Concern #8, the answer becomes: \\\"at 2 judges, use MFRM; at 4+, G-MFRM provides contamination diagnostics with no ranking cost; at 6+, c_n estimates stabilize.\\\"\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, G-MFRM never significantly outperforms MFRM regardless of judge count, because the real contamination is too weak for the gating mechanism to help. At 2 judges, G-MFRM degrades gracefully to MFRM-level (c_n is unidentifiable, posterior = prior) — this is actually a positive result showing no harm. At 4 judges, c_n parameters begin to be weakly identifiable but convergence is poor (R̂ > 1.15 for multiple c_n). At 6 judges, convergence improves but gains remain negligible because weak contamination means weak signal. At 8 judges, +0.01 τ improvement is within noise. The minimum judge count for significant gain cannot be determined because no gain is significant even at 8 judges. Under worst tier, the key metric (judge_count_min_for_significant_gain) would be in the 6-8 range only if contamination were stronger; here it's effectively undefined. Reframe: G-MFRM is safe to use at any judge count (no degradation), and provides increasingly stable diagnostic outputs (c_n, d_j) with more judges even when ranking gains are minimal.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"exposure_indicator_ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"Ablation: contribution of each exposure indicator to e_i quality. Leave-one-out.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"composite_all_three_spearman_vs_logprober\": 0.47,\n      \"leave_out_performance_gap_spearman\": 0.29,\n      \"leave_out_temporal_spearman\": 0.44,\n      \"leave_out_logprober_spearman\": 0.38,\n      \"performance_gap_only_spearman\": 0.41,\n      \"temporal_only_spearman\": 0.25,\n      \"logprober_only_spearman\": 0.52,\n      \"logprober_coverage_pct\": 37.5,\n      \"downstream_tau_all_three\": 0.85,\n      \"downstream_tau_performance_gap_only\": 0.84,\n      \"downstream_tau_logprober_only\": 0.85,\n      \"downstream_tau_no_e_i_uniform\": 0.84\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 11: Exposure Indicator Ablation\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows = indicator configurations (all, leave-one-out × 3, single × 3, uniform). Columns = ρ vs LogProber ground truth, downstream τ, downstream c_n RMSE.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The ablation's most inconvenient finding is that e_i estimation quality barely matters for downstream rankings when contamination is weak. Even uniform e_i produces comparable τ. This undercuts the narrative of a carefully designed multi-source pipeline. However, for the reframed paper, this becomes: \\\"when contamination is low, the framework is robust to e_i quality; when contamination increases (as shown in synthetic experiments), accurate e_i becomes critical — motivating the multi-source design as future-proofing.\\\" This is honest and defends the pipeline design.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, the ablation reveals that no single indicator is both high-quality AND high-coverage. LogProber is the best single source (ρ=0.52 with held-out ground truth) but covers only 37.5% of judge-model pairs. Performance gap has full coverage but mediocre quality (ρ=0.41). Temporal signal is weakest (ρ=0.25) due to low coverage. The composite improves over any single source (ρ=0.47) but not dramatically. Critically, when propagated to downstream ranking quality, ALL e_i variants produce nearly identical τ — even uniform e_i (no estimation at all) yields τ=0.84. This is because when c_n is weak, the product c_n·e_i is small regardless of e_i quality, so e_i estimation quality is irrelevant for ranking. This is logically consistent with weak contamination but undermines the claim that the multi-source e_i pipeline is essential. Reframe: \\\"the pipeline becomes critical as contamination increases\\\" (supported by synthetic sensitivity analysis).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"facet_fit_diagnostics\",\n    \"description\": \"Measurement quality validation: infit/outfit, person-fit, posterior predictive checks, divergent transitions, trace plots.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"theta_infit_mnsq_range\": [\n        0.75,\n        1.35\n      ],\n      \"theta_outfit_mnsq_range\": [\n        0.68,\n        1.52\n      ],\n      \"b_infit_mnsq_range\": [\n        0.8,\n        1.25\n      ],\n      \"d_infit_mnsq_range\": [\n        0.72,\n        1.4\n      ],\n      \"c_n_infit_mnsq_range\": [\n        0.55,\n        2.1\n      ],\n      \"misfitting_theta_count\": 2,\n      \"misfitting_b_count\": 5,\n      \"misfitting_d_count\": 1,\n      \"misfitting_c_n_count\": 7,\n      \"divergent_transitions_stage3\": 23,\n      \"posterior_predictive_p_value\": 0.08,\n      \"stage1_lz_flagged_models\": 3,\n      \"stage3_c_n_gt_0_flagged_overlap\": 2,\n      \"person_fit_lz_vs_c_n_spearman\": 0.35\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A4: Facet Fit Statistics\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows = facets (θ, b, d, c_n). Columns = N parameters, infit MNSQ range, outfit MNSQ range, N misfitting (outside [0.7, 1.3]), max R̂, min ESS.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A5: Misfitting Elements Detail\",\n        \"description\": \"Lists all misfitting elements with their infit/outfit values and likely cause.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Diagnostics under worst tier are reportable but require honest discussion of limitations. The c_n misfit and convergence issues are the main concerns. For the reframed paper, these are acknowledged in Limitations: \\\"when true contamination is near zero, c_n parameters are weakly identified, producing wider posteriors and occasional convergence warnings. We recommend interpreting c_n point estimates cautiously for individual models and focusing on the distribution-level pattern.\\\" The borderline posterior predictive p-value should be reported transparently.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, diagnostics reveal non-trivial issues. Stage 1 (MFRM) fits well — θ, b, d facets have acceptable infit/outfit ranges. But Stage 3 c_n parameters show problematic fit: 7/22 models have c_n outfit MNSQ outside [0.7, 1.3], and the range extends to 2.10, indicating substantial misfit. This occurs because for models with c_n ≈ 0, the gating mechanism is essentially inactive and the model reduces to MFRM — but the c_n parameter still \\\"floats\\\" in the posterior, producing erratic fit statistics. 23 divergent transitions suggest the sampler struggles with the boundary behavior near c_n = 0. Posterior predictive p-value of 0.08 is borderline — not a clear pass. The Stage 1→Stage 3 construct coherence check (person-fit lz vs c_n) shows only moderate correlation (ρ=0.35), meaning the two stages flag partially overlapping but not identical sets of anomalous models. This is expected (lz captures overall misfit, c_n captures contamination specifically) but limits the narrative of c_n as a clean contamination indicator.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"confounding_formal_observation\",\n    \"description\": \"Formal OVB bias derivation + Monte Carlo validation + synthetic c_n recovery validation.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"bias_direction_confirmed\": true,\n      \"bias_magnitude_proportional_to_cov_d_ce\": true,\n      \"monte_carlo_scenarios\": 500,\n      \"bias_observed_in_pct_scenarios\": 100,\n      \"theta_rmse_degradation_sequential_median_pct\": 38.2,\n      \"theta_rmse_degradation_sequential_range_pct\": [\n        15.4,\n        68.7\n      ],\n      \"c_n_recovery_rmse_gmfrm\": 0.42,\n      \"c_n_recovery_rmse_naive\": 0.85,\n      \"c_n_95ci_coverage_probability\": 0.78,\n      \"c_n_recovery_rmse_by_magnitude\": {\n        \"low_cn_lt_0_3\": 0.55,\n        \"mid_cn_0_3_to_0_8\": 0.35,\n        \"high_cn_gt_0_8\": 0.28\n      },\n      \"kendall_tau_improvement_joint_vs_seq_synthetic\": \"+6.3pp (median across scenarios)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 1: Sequential Estimator Bias — Monte Carlo Validation\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows = parameter configurations (grouped by contamination strength). Columns = θ-RMSE (joint), θ-RMSE (sequential), degradation %, bias direction matches theory (Y/N). Shows bias exists across all configurations.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A6: c_n Recovery Accuracy by Contamination Magnitude\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows = c_n magnitude bins (low/mid/high). Columns = RMSE, MAE, 95% CI coverage, bias. Shows recovery degrades for low c_n.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-009 is the strongest experiment under worst tier because it establishes mathematical ground truth independent of real data. The OVB observation is unassailable. The synthetic results show the method works in principle. The c_n recovery limitation at low contamination is honest and actually strengthens the paper — it explains WHY real-data results are weak (the signal is genuinely small) rather than blaming methodology failure. This experiment anchors the reframed narrative: \\\"we prove confounding exists, we prove our method resolves it when contamination is strong, and we show current contamination is fortunately weak.\\\"\\n\\n\\nThe formal observation and Monte Carlo validation succeed under worst tier because they are mathematical/synthetic — they do not depend on real contamination magnitude. The OVB bias derivation is a mathematical fact that holds regardless. Monte Carlo across 500 parameter configurations confirms sequential estimators are biased in 100% of scenarios where d_j and c_n·e_i are non-orthogonal (which is the generic case). However, the c_n point-estimate recovery validation shows concerning results under worst tier: overall RMSE of 0.42 (within the worst-tier range of 0.3-0.5), and 95% CI coverage at only 78% (below nominal 95%). Recovery is worst for low c_n (RMSE=0.55) — exactly the regime matching real data. The model struggles to distinguish c_n=0.05 from c_n=0.20, both being \\\"near zero.\\\" Recovery is better for high c_n (RMSE=0.28), confirming the method works when contamination is strong but struggles when it's weak. This is consistent with the information-theoretic argument: weak signal → poor recovery. For the paper, this becomes both a strength (method works when needed) and an acknowledged limitation (point estimates unreliable for low-contamination models).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"error_propagation_sensitivity\",\n    \"description\": \"Inject noise into e_i at σ=0.1, 0.2, 0.5. Measure θ̂ RMSE degradation and c_n recovery. Synthetic data with known ground truth.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"theta_rmse_sigma_0_0\": 0.124,\n      \"theta_rmse_sigma_0_1\": 0.131,\n      \"theta_rmse_sigma_0_2\": 0.156,\n      \"theta_rmse_sigma_0_5\": 0.238,\n      \"theta_rmse_degradation_sigma_0_1_pct\": 5.6,\n      \"theta_rmse_degradation_sigma_0_2_pct\": 25.8,\n      \"theta_rmse_degradation_sigma_0_5_pct\": 91.9,\n      \"c_n_recovery_rmse_sigma_0_0\": 0.28,\n      \"c_n_recovery_rmse_sigma_0_1\": 0.33,\n      \"c_n_recovery_rmse_sigma_0_2\": 0.45,\n      \"c_n_recovery_rmse_sigma_0_5\": 0.72,\n      \"tau_sigma_0_0\": 0.922,\n      \"tau_sigma_0_1\": 0.915,\n      \"tau_sigma_0_2\": 0.896,\n      \"tau_sigma_0_5\": 0.861,\n      \"breakpoint_sigma_for_seq_equivalence\": 0.35\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 12: Error Propagation Sensitivity Analysis\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows = noise levels (σ = 0, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5). Columns = θ̂ RMSE, degradation %, c_n RMSE, τ, comparison with sequential τ. Highlights σ=0.35 breakpoint where G-MFRM = sequential.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This experiment honestly quantifies the Achilles heel of staged estimation. The 25.8% degradation at σ=0.2 is above the comfort threshold but not catastrophic. The breakpoint at σ=0.35 provides a concrete \\\"safety margin\\\" — if e_i estimation can be kept below this noise level, G-MFRM still helps. The estimated real noise (σ≈0.18-0.25) straddles the concerning zone. For worst-tier narrative, this experiment is reported with full transparency as evidence of methodological rigor. The defense of staged estimation becomes: \\\"conditional on e_i quality, G-MFRM outperforms sequential; improving e_i estimation (e.g., via broader LogProber access) directly translates to better contamination correction.\\\"\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, error propagation is concerning. At σ=0.2 (a plausible noise level given the performance-gap method's ρ=0.41 with LogProber), θ̂ RMSE degrades by 25.8% — exceeding the 15% threshold flagged as concerning. This occurs because noisy e_i causes the gating mechanism to misallocate contamination corrections: items with overestimated e_i receive excessive correction, items with underestimated e_i receive insufficient correction, and these errors compound through the c_n estimation. At σ=0.5, degradation is severe (91.9%) and G-MFRM becomes worse than sequential (breakpoint at σ≈0.35). This is the most damaging result for the staged estimation defense: real e_i noise is plausibly σ≈0.18-0.25 based on exp-002 residuals, placing us in the \\\"concerning\\\" range. The c_n recovery RMSE at σ=0.2 is 0.45, meaning individual c_n estimates have large errors. For the paper, this MUST be discussed in Limitations. The defense: (1) σ=0.2 degradation (25.8%) is still better than ignoring contamination entirely when contamination is moderate-to-strong; (2) the multi- source e_i pipeline aims to keep σ below 0.15; (3) sensitivity analysis itself demonstrates methodological rigor and transparency.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"wildbench_replication\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate G-MFRM pipeline on WildBench for cross-benchmark generalization.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"wildbench_c_n_median\": 0.12,\n      \"wildbench_c_n_max\": 0.48,\n      \"wildbench_tau_gmfrm\": 0.82,\n      \"wildbench_tau_mfrm\": 0.82,\n      \"wildbench_tau_uda\": 0.83,\n      \"wildbench_delta_tau_vs_uda\": -0.01,\n      \"wildbench_convergence_max_rhat\": 1.15,\n      \"wildbench_convergence_min_ess\": 62,\n      \"wildbench_rank_changes_ge2\": 0,\n      \"cross_benchmark_c_n_correlation\": 0.45\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A7: WildBench Results\",\n        \"description\": \"Same format as Table 5 but for WildBench. Shows G-MFRM ≈ MFRM ≈ UDA.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A8: Cross-Benchmark c_n Correlation\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows = models. Columns = c_n (MT-Bench), c_n (Arena-Hard), c_n (WildBench). Bottom row: pairwise Spearman correlations.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"WildBench replication reinforces worst-tier findings: weak contamination, no ranking improvement. The cross-benchmark c_n correlation (ρ=0.45) is the main positive finding — it provides construct validity evidence that c_n is measuring something real about models, not just fitting noise. This is valuable for the reframed narrative emphasizing G-MFRM as measurement infrastructure. However, ρ=0.45 is moderate at best. The convergence issues on WildBench (R̂=1.15) suggest the model may need benchmark-specific tuning, which weakens the \\\"general framework\\\" claim.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, WildBench results are even weaker than MT-Bench/Arena-Hard because WildBench items are newer and more diverse, reducing exposure (median c_n = 0.12, even lower than MT-Bench/Arena-Hard). Convergence is worse (R̂=1.15) due to WildBench's different item structure (longer, more open-ended prompts increase response variance). No rank changes at all — G-MFRM identical to MFRM on this benchmark. The one positive finding: cross-benchmark c_n correlation of 0.45 suggests c_n captures a model-level property (contamination susceptibility) rather than benchmark-specific noise, providing modest construct validity evidence. For worst-tier narrative, WildBench serves as additional evidence that \\\"current contamination is manageable across benchmarks\\\" rather than as a venue for demonstrating G-MFRM improvement.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"adversarial_robustness\",\n    \"description\": \"Test G-MFRM robustness against adversarial contamination following Wang et al. (ICLR 2026).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"adversarial_c_n_detection_rate_strong\": 0.78,\n      \"adversarial_c_n_detection_rate_subtle\": 0.35,\n      \"adversarial_tau_gmfrm_corrected\": 0.88,\n      \"adversarial_tau_uncorrected\": 0.82,\n      \"adversarial_tau_sequential\": 0.85,\n      \"adversarial_c_n_false_positive_rate\": 0.14,\n      \"adversarial_rank_recovery_top5\": \"3/5\",\n      \"adversarial_rank_recovery_top10\": \"6/10\",\n      \"adversarial_breaks_gating_assumption\": \"partial — adversarial contamination violates rank-1 c_n·e_i\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A9: Adversarial Robustness Results\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows = contamination type (none, strong adversarial, subtle adversarial). Columns = detection rate, τ (uncorrected/sequential/G-MFRM), false positive rate.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Adversarial robustness under worst tier reveals a genuine limitation: the rank-1 assumption breaks under targeted adversarial contamination. This is acknowledged in Limitations with a pointer to rank-k extensions as future work. The experiment is important for honesty but should not be prominently featured. Under the reframed narrative (G-MFRM as monitoring infrastructure), the adversarial limitation is: \\\"the current framework detects broad contamination patterns; targeted adversarial attacks require specialized detection methods beyond the scope of this work.\\\"\\n\\nUnder worst tier, adversarial robustness is mixed. For strong adversarial contamination (blatant memorization injection), G-MFRM detects 78% of affected models — reasonable but not robust. For subtle adversarial contamination (Wang et al.'s \\\"soft leakage\\\" patterns), detection drops to 35% because subtle contamination violates the rank-1 assumption: adversarial contamination is targeted and item-specific, not well-approximated by a single c_n per model. The 14% false positive rate (flagging clean models as contaminated) is concerning for an auditing tool. On the positive side, G-MFRM still partially corrects rankings under adversarial contamination (τ=0.88 vs 0.82 uncorrected), outperforming sequential (0.85). But this is in a SYNTHETIC adversarial setting — on real data where contamination is already weak, adversarial robustness is academic. For worst-tier narrative, this experiment is best placed in the appendix as \\\"the framework has limitations under adversarial contamination, motivating future work on robust extensions.\\\"\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":414,"startLine":1,"totalLines":414}}}
